## Given and unit conversions

\[
L=1.00\ \mathrm{m},\qquad P_{\text{total}}=30.0\ \mathrm{kN}=3.00\times10^4\ \mathrm{N}
\]

Aluminum:

\[
A_{\mathrm{Al}}=200\ \mathrm{mm^2}=2.00\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^2}
\]

\[
E_{\mathrm{Al}}=70.0\ \mathrm{GPa}=70.0\times10^9\ \mathrm{Pa}
\]

Steel:

\[
A_{\mathrm{St}}=100\ \mathrm{mm^2}=1.00\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^2}
\]

\[
E_{\mathrm{St}}=200\ \mathrm{GPa}=200\times10^9\ \mathrm{Pa}
\]

## Compatibility and equilibrium

Because the bars are connected between rigid end plates, they undergo the same extension:

\[
\delta_{\mathrm{Al}}=\delta_{\mathrm{St}}=\delta
\]

For an axially loaded linear-elastic bar,

\[
F=\frac{EA}{L}\delta
\]

Thus, the axial stiffnesses are

\[
k_{\mathrm{Al}}=\frac{E_{\mathrm{Al}}A_{\mathrm{Al}}}{L}
=\frac{(70.0\times10^9)(2.00\times10^{-4})}{1.00}
=14.0\times10^6\ \mathrm{N/m}
\]

\[
k_{\mathrm{St}}=\frac{E_{\mathrm{St}}A_{\mathrm{St}}}{L}
=\frac{(200\times10^9)(1.00\times10^{-4})}{1.00}
=20.0\times10^6\ \mathrm{N/m}
\]

The total stiffness is

\[
k_{\text{total}}=k_{\mathrm{Al}}+k_{\mathrm{St}}
=14.0\times10^6+20.0\times10^6
=34.0\times10^6\ \mathrm{N/m}
\]

Equilibrium requires

\[
P_{\text{total}}=F_{\mathrm{Al}}+F_{\mathrm{St}}
=(k_{\mathrm{Al}}+k_{\mathrm{St}})\delta
\]

Therefore,

\[
\delta=\frac{3.00\times10^4}{34.0\times10^6}
=8.8235\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m}
\]

or

\[
\delta=0.88235\ \mathrm{mm}
\]

The common axial strain is

\[
\varepsilon=\frac{\delta}{L}
=\frac{8.8235\times10^{-4}}{1.00}
=8.8235\times10^{-4}
\]

## Force in each bar

Aluminum:

\[
F_{\mathrm{Al}}=k_{\mathrm{Al}}\delta
=(14.0\times10^6)(8.8235\times10^{-4})
=1.2353\times10^4\ \mathrm{N}
\]

\[
F_{\mathrm{Al}}=12.35\ \mathrm{kN}
\]

Steel:

\[
F_{\mathrm{St}}=k_{\mathrm{St}}\delta
=(20.0\times10^6)(8.8235\times10^{-4})
=1.7647\times10^4\ \mathrm{N}
\]

\[
F_{\mathrm{St}}=17.65\ \mathrm{kN}
\]

Check:

\[
12.35+17.65=30.0\ \mathrm{kN}
\]

## Stress in each bar

Aluminum:

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{Al}}=\frac{F_{\mathrm{Al}}}{A_{\mathrm{Al}}}
=\frac{1.2353\times10^4}{2.00\times10^{-4}}
=6.1765\times10^7\ \mathrm{Pa}
\]

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{Al}}=61.8\ \mathrm{MPa}
\]

Steel:

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{St}}=\frac{F_{\mathrm{St}}}{A_{\mathrm{St}}}
=\frac{1.7647\times10^4}{1.00\times10^{-4}}
=1.7647\times10^8\ \mathrm{Pa}
\]

\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{St}}=176\ \mathrm{MPa}
\]

Both stresses are tensile.

## Final conclusion

\[
\boxed{\delta_{\mathrm{Al}}=\delta_{\mathrm{St}}=0.882\ \mathrm{mm}}
\]

\[
\boxed{\sigma_{\mathrm{Al}}=61.8\ \mathrm{MPa}\ \text{(tension)}}
\]

\[
\boxed{\sigma_{\mathrm{St}}=176\ \mathrm{MPa}\ \text{(tension)}}
\]

\[
\boxed{F_{\mathrm{Al}}=12.4\ \mathrm{kN}\ \text{(tension)}}
\]

\[
\boxed{F_{\mathrm{St}}=17.6\ \mathrm{kN}\ \text{(tension)}}
\]

Assumptions used: the end plates are rigid and enforce equal extensions; the bars are prismatic, homogeneous, initially unstressed, and axially loaded; stress and strain are uniform over each cross-section; deformations are small and linear elastic; there is no temperature change, connection slip, buckling, or load eccentricity.

Assumptions used, all from the prompt: constant \(c\), perfect mixing, all heater power enters the liquid, no heat loss, negligible vessel heat capacity, and equality with \(120\,\mathrm{s}\) is allowed. A Celsius temperature difference equals the same difference in kelvin.

## 1. Energy requirement

\[
\Delta T = 35.0-20.0 = 15.0\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}=15.0\,\mathrm{K}
\]

\[
Q = mc\Delta T
= (2.00\,\mathrm{kg})\left(4000\,\frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{kg\,K}}\right)(15.0\,\mathrm{K})
\]

\[
mc = 8000\,\frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{K}}
\]

\[
Q = 8000 \times 15.0 = 120000\,\mathrm{J}
=1.20\times10^5\,\mathrm{J}=120\,\mathrm{kJ}
\]

## 2. Selected heater and heating time

Minimum average power needed to meet the limit:

\[
P_{\min}=\frac{Q}{t_{\max}}
=\frac{120000\,\mathrm{J}}{120\,\mathrm{s}}
=1000\,\mathrm{W}
\]

Check available heaters:

\[
t_{750}=\frac{120000}{750}=160\,\mathrm{s}>120\,\mathrm{s}
\quad\text{fails}
\]

\[
t_{1000}=\frac{120000}{1000}=120\,\mathrm{s}=t_{\max}
\quad\text{passes because equality is allowed}
\]

\[
t_{1250}=\frac{120000}{1250}=96.0\,\mathrm{s}<120\,\mathrm{s}
\quad\text{passes, but it is not the smallest passing power}
\]

So the smallest passing heater is:

\[
\boxed{1000\,\mathrm{W}}
\]

For the selected heater,

\[
\frac{\Delta T}{\Delta t}=\frac{P}{mc}
=\frac{1000}{8000}=0.125\,\mathrm{K/s}
\]

\[
t=\frac{15.0\,\mathrm{K}}{0.125\,\mathrm{K/s}}=120\,\mathrm{s}
\]

It must be switched off at \(t=120\,\mathrm{s}\), when the mixed liquid reaches \(35.0\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The next smaller heater, \(750\,\mathrm{W}\), would need \(160\,\mathrm{s}\), exceeding the limit by \(40\,\mathrm{s}\).

## 3. Energy delivered by the selected heater

The selected \(1000\,\mathrm{W}\) heater is on for \(120\,\mathrm{s}\):

\[
E_{\text{delivered}}=Pt
=(1000\,\mathrm{W})(120\,\mathrm{s})
=120000\,\mathrm{J}
\]

\[
E_{\text{delivered}}=1.20\times10^5\,\mathrm{J}=120\,\mathrm{kJ}
\]

This equals the required energy \(Q\), consistent with no heat loss and negligible vessel heat capacity.

## Final conclusion

The liquid requires \(\boxed{1.20\times10^5\,\mathrm{J}}\) \(\boxed{(120\,\mathrm{kJ})}\). Choose the \(\boxed{1000\,\mathrm{W}}\) heater: it reaches \(35.0\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in exactly \(\boxed{120\,\mathrm{s}}\), which passes because equality is allowed, and it should be switched off then. The next smaller heater, \(\boxed{750\,\mathrm{W}}\), would need \(\boxed{160\,\mathrm{s}}\) and fails. The selected heater delivers \(\boxed{1.20\times10^5\,\mathrm{J}}\) before switch-off.
